<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD) Clinical Guide</title>
    <style>
        :root {
            --primary-dark: #0f172a;
            --medical-blue: #0284c7;
            --medical-light: #f0f9ff;
            --text-main: #334155;
            --border-subtle: #cbd5e1;
            --accent-purple: #7c3aed;
            --accent-amber: #d97706;
            --accent-rose: #e11d48;
        }

        body {
            font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, "Segoe UI", Roboto, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;
            line-height: 1.6;
            color: var(--text-main);
            background-color: #f8fafc;
            margin: 0;
            padding: 24px;
        }

        .container {
            max-width: 1050px;
            margin: 0 auto;
            background: #ffffff;
            padding: 45px;
            border-radius: 16px;
            box-shadow: 0 10px 25px -5px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.05), 0 8px 10px -6px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.05);
        }

        header {
            border-bottom: 4px solid var(--medical-blue);
            padding-bottom: 20px;
            margin-bottom: 35px;
        }

        h1 {
            color: var(--primary-dark);
            margin: 0 0 10px 0;
            font-size: 2.6rem;
            font-weight: 800;
            letter-spacing: -0.025em;
        }

        .subtitle {
            font-size: 1.15rem;
            color: #64748b;
            margin: 0;
        }

        h2 {
            color: var(--primary-dark);
            font-size: 1.6rem;
            margin-top: 40px;
            margin-bottom: 20px;
            display: flex;
            align-items: center;
        }

        h2::before {
            content: "";
            display: inline-block;
            width: 5px;
            height: 24px;
            background-color: var(--medical-blue);
            margin-right: 12px;
            border-radius: 4px;
        }

        p {
            margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
            text-align: justify;
        }

        /* Flexbox/Table Layout for Dual Pathologies */
        .pathology-box {
            display: table;
            width: 100%;
            margin: 25px 0;
            border-collapse: separate;
            border-spacing: 20px 0;
        }

        .pathology-column {
            display: table-cell;
            background: var(--medical-light);
            border: 1px solid #e0f2fe;
            border-radius: 12px;
            padding: 25px;
            width: 50%;
            vertical-align: top;
        }

        .pathology-column h3 {
            margin-top: 0;
            color: var(--medical-blue);
            font-size: 1.25rem;
        }

        /* Staging Table Layout */
        .table-container {
            overflow-x: auto;
            margin: 25px 0;
            border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);
            border-radius: 8px;
        }

        table {
            width: 100%;
            border-collapse: collapse;
            text-align: left;
            font-size: 0.95rem;
        }

        th {
            background-color: var(--primary-dark);
            color: #ffffff;
            padding: 14px 16px;
            font-weight: 600;
        }

        td {
            padding: 14px 16px;
            border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);
        }

        tr:last-child td {
            border-bottom: none;
        }

        .stage-badge {
            display: inline-block;
            padding: 4px 10px;
            border-radius: 6px;
            font-weight: 700;
            font-size: 0.85rem;
            text-align: center;
        }

        .badge-1 { background-color: #dcfce7; color: #16a34a; }
        .badge-2 { background-color: #fef9c3; color: #ca8a04; }
        .badge-3a { background-color: #ffedd5; color: #ea580c; }
        .badge-3b { background-color: #fee2e2; color: #dc2626; }
        .badge-4 { background-color: #f3e8ff; color: #7c3aed; }
        .badge-5 { background-color: #ffe4e6; color: #be123c; }

        /* Systemic Complications List Layout */
        .systems-container {
            display: block;
            margin: 25px 0;
        }

        .system-card {
            border-left: 4px solid var(--border-subtle);
            padding-left: 20px;
            margin-bottom: 20px;
        }

        .system-card.cv { border-left-color: var(--accent-rose); }
        .system-card.hem { border-left-color: var(--accent-amber); }
        .system-card.bone { border-left-color: var(--accent-purple); }

        .system-title {
            font-weight: 700;
            color: var(--primary-dark);
            margin-bottom: 4px;
        }

        /* Management Blocks */
        .mgmt-grid {
            margin: 25px 0;
        }

        .mgmt-item {
            background: #f8fafc;
            border: 1px solid #e2e8f0;
            border-radius: 8px;
            padding: 16px 20px;
            margin-bottom: 12px;
        }

        .mgmt-title {
            font-weight: bold;
            color: var(--primary-dark);
        }

        @media (max-width: 768px) {
            .container { padding: 24px; }
            .pathology-box, .pathology-column { display: block; width: 100%; }
            .pathology-column { margin-bottom: 20px; }
            h1 { font-size: 2rem; }
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>

<div class="container">
    <header>
        <h1>Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD)</h1>
        <div class="subtitle">Clinical Manifestations, Pathophysiology, and KDIGO Staging</div>
    </header>

    <p><strong>Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD)</strong> is defined as the presence of kidney damage or a sustained reduction in the Glomerular Filtration Rate (GFR) lasting for <strong>greater than 3 months</strong>. Unlike Acute Kidney Injury, CKD involves the slow, irreversible destruction of nephrons, leading to structural remodeling, systemic uremia, and progressive renal insufficiency.</p>

    <h2>1. Primary Etiology & Pathophysiology</h2>
    <p>When operational nephrons are destroyed, the surviving cohort undergoes compensatory hyperfiltration. While this response temporarily meets clearance demands, the sustained hemodynamic strain triggers glomerular sclerosis, initiating a cycle of continuous nephron drop-out.</p>

    <div class="pathology-box">
        <div class="pathology-column">
            <h3>Diabetic Nephropathy</h3>
            <p>Chronic hyperglycemia initiates non-enzymatic glycosylation of the glomerular basement membrane. This causes efferent arteriole vasoconstriction, severe intraglomerular hypertension, and podocyte damage, eventually advancing to diffuse glomerulosclerosis.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pathology-column">
            <h3>Hypertensive Nephrosclerosis</h3>
            <p>Sustained systemic hypertension transmits mechanical stress into the renal vasculature, resulting in hyaline arteriolosclerosis. The narrowing of the lumen directly limits perfusion, causing ischemic injury, tubular atrophy, and interstitial fibrosis.</p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2>2. KDIGO Staging Framework</h2>
    <p>Clinical tracking and interventions are guided by the Estimated Glomerular Filtration Rate (eGFR), scaled to normal body surface area ($mL/min/1.73m^2$):</p>

    <div class="table-container">
        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>Stage</th>
                    <th>eGFR Range</th>
                    <th>Clinical Designation</th>
                    <th>Therapeutic Focus</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td><span class="stage-badge badge-1">Stage 1</span></td>
                    <td><strong>&ge; 90</strong></td>
                    <td>Kidney damage with normal or elevated GFR</td>
                    <td>Diagnosis, treatment of primary cause, slowing progression.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><span class="stage-badge badge-2">Stage 2</span></td>
                    <td><strong>60 &ndash; 89</strong></td>
                    <td>Mildly decreased GFR</td>
                    <td>Estimation of progression rate; monitoring proteinuria.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><span class="stage-badge badge-3a">Stage 3a</span></td>
                    <td><strong>45 &ndash; 59</strong></td>
                    <td>Mildly to moderately decreased GFR</td>
                    <td>Evaluating and treating systemic complications.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><span class="stage-badge badge-3b">Stage 3b</span></td>
                    <td><strong>30 &ndash; 44</strong></td>
                    <td>Moderately to severely decreased GFR</td>
                    <td>Aggressive complication management; dosage adjustments.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><span class="stage-badge badge-4">Stage 4</span></td>
                    <td><strong>15 &ndash; 29</strong></td>
                    <td>Severely decreased GFR</td>
                    <td>Preparation for Renal Replacement Therapy (RRT) / Dialysis.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><span class="stage-badge badge-5">Stage 5</span></td>
                    <td><strong>&lt; 15</strong></td>
                    <td>End-Stage Renal Disease (ESRD)</td>
                    <td>Kidney failure. Urgent maintenance RRT or transplantation.</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <h2>3. Multisystem Complications</h2>
    <p>Advanced GFR reduction alters regulatory, excretory, and endocrine systems uniformly across the body:</p>

    <div class="systems-container">
        <div class="system-card cv">
            <div class="system-title">Cardiovascular System (Fluid Overload & Hypertension)</div>
            <p>Decreased sodium and water filtration expands intravascular volume, driving peripheral edema, pulmonary congestion, left ventricular hypertrophy, and accelerated cardiovascular disease.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="system-card hem">
            <div class="system-title">Hematologic System (Normocytic Anemia)</div>
            <p>Peritubular fibroblasts in the renal cortex fail to produce sufficient <strong>erythropoietin (EPO)</strong>, reducing bone marrow stimulation and presenting as a normocytic, normochromic anemia.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="system-card bone">
            <div class="system-title">Mineral and Bone Disorder (CKD-MBD)</div>
            <p>Diminished 1-alpha-hydroxylase activity prevents the activation of Vitamin D, reducing intestinal calcium absorption (hypocalcemia). Concurrently, phosphorus retention (hyperphosphatemia) drives parathyroid gland hyperplasia. Elevated Parathyroid Hormone (PTH) strips skeletal calcium, resulting in renal osteodystrophy.</p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2>4. Core Clinical Management</h2>
    <div class="mgmt-grid">
        <div class="mgmt-item">
            <span class="mgmt-title">Renoprotective Hemodynamics:</span> Interrupting the RAAS axis with ACE inhibitors or Angiotensin Receptor Blockers (ARBs) to lower efferent arteriolar tone, reduce protein spillover, and control systemic blood pressure.
        </div>
        <div class="mgmt-item">
            <span class="mgmt-title">Dietary Substrate Alterations:</span> Restriction of dietary sodium, fluid volume limits, and strict constraints on potassium and phosphorus intake to avoid systemic toxic accumulation.
        </div>
        <div class="mgmt-item">
            <span class="mgmt-title">Phosphate Binders:</span> Administering oral phosphate-binding agents (e.g., sevelamer, calcium acetate) alongside food intake to inhibit gastrointestinal absorption of dietary phosphorus.
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

</body>
</html>
